Z||000000||Gold87SE||04-28-2001||12:47 AM||||First I started out with a 87 se that died at 140,000 miles and then had a rebuilt engine put in which lasted 4,000 miles and needed to be rebuilt again and lasted another 4,000 miles. <P>Just today I got an 87 GT. It has a problem where it holds out first gear for a while. I had it for 4 hours and now it's dead and won't start back up. It shifted and then died. When I try to start it up, the engine will just shake. I don't know what to do.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spektyr||04-28-2001||12:52 AM||||Give us some more info.  I'm not a Fiero God, but even the most holy of the Gods couldn't give much advice without more info.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-28-2001||01:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Gold87SE:<BR><B>First I started out with a 87 se that died at 140,000 miles and then had a rebuilt engine put in which lasted 4,000 miles and needed to be rebuilt again and lasted another 4,000 miles. <P>Just today I got an 87 GT. It has a problem where it holds out first gear for a while. I had it for 4 hours and now it's dead and won't start back up. It shifted and then died. When I try to start it up, the engine will just shake. I don't know what to do.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If it's an automatic, disconnect the TCC connector. It might be stuck in the locked mode.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold87SE||04-28-2001||01:02 AM||||Well, everything engine wise seemed to be ok when I got the car. The only problem was with the shifting. The car holds out really long in first and then goes into second pretty hard. I was driving it and it was doing this like it normally does and when it slammed into second, it just died.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold87SE||04-28-2001||01:16 AM||||Oreif, If this is the case, could it have possibly blow a fuse?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||04-28-2001||06:01 AM||||<BR>You are driving an automatic and the torque converter lockup is locked when it souldn't be...<BR>Try unplugging it...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||04-28-2001||09:07 AM||||Yup, disconnect the TCC <A HREF="http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html</A>  <P>Also, shift speeds are controled by the cable from the throttle to the transmission (TV cable). Its adjustable but not likely to be your problem.<P>Quality of shift is controlled by the vacume modulator which is about $10. The ones you get as a replacement are adjustable between harsh and soft shifts. These things do go bad and need replacement but look for a vacume leak first as even a good one needs the right vacume to work properly.<P>BUT the very first thing you do is unplug the TCC. You can drive forever with out it. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gold87SE||04-28-2001||09:18 AM||||I got it started. It keeps blowing the fuel pump fuse. It's even doing it at idle now.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SteveJ||04-28-2001||09:34 AM||||OK blown fuses on the pump explain the engine stall you described earlier folowing the shift to second.<BR>Takes a few minutes to blow the fuse? I'd have to say the pump itself has a problem. Too bad, its a nusance to change 'cause its in the tank. If it had a short in the wiring somewhere it would blow immediately. Do you have to crank a long time to get it to start? If so maybe you have a short in the wiring in or around the fuel pump relay and the engine starts when the oil pressure completes the circut to the pump.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||04-28-2001||11:37 AM||||That might also explain why your engines only last 4000 miles - if the fuel pump isn't delivering enough fuel you might be running lean. I though there were other safegaurds in the controls to prevent this type of damage??||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||04-28-2001||12:03 PM||||Your new one definatly has a pump bad or the wiring going to it. Check the wiring to the engine oil pressure switch as well. You could have a section rubbed thru.<P>The old one may have had fuel problem as well.<P>running lean can kill motors real quick. The ECM can only accomodate for so much of it. You big problem is at WOT. As I understand it... The ECM ignores the O2 sensor then. You could easily run lean and cook the motor. TerryK could tell you better about the ECM programming and what it does when...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||1
